The '''2014 FIFA Club World Cup''' (officially known as the '''FIFA Club World Cup Morocco 2014 presented by Toyota''' for sponsorship reasons)<ref name="schedule">{{cite web|url=http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/33/87/09/fcwc2014_matchschedule_05112014_neutral.pdf|title=Match Schedule – FIFA Club World Cup Morocco 2014|publisher=FIFA.com|access-date=9 December 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141203005236/http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/33/87/09/fcwc2014_matchschedule_05112014_neutral.pdf|archive-date=3 December 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> was the 11th edition of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]], a [[FIFA]]-organised international club [[association football|football]] tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations as well as the host nation's league champions.<ref name="regulations">{{cite web|url=http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/33/64/35/clubwc2014e_neutral.pdf|title=Regulations – FIFA Club World Cup Morocco 2014|publisher=FIFA|access-date=9 December 2014|archive-date=3 December 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141203004255/http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/33/64/35/clubwc2014e_neutral.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> It was hosted by Morocco for the second consecutive season,<ref>{{cite news | url = https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5j8XZ6B1erRErqNmo04nPMLK4CMvQ?docId=CNG.cc0deb74231895b25c32fdd9a671f34f.4a1 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20140218222622/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5j8XZ6B1erRErqNmo04nPMLK4CMvQ?docId=CNG.cc0deb74231895b25c32fdd9a671f34f.4a1 | url-status = dead | archive-date = 18 February 2014 | title = Morocco to host 2013-2014 Club World Cup | publisher = Google News | agency = Agence France-Presse| date = 17 December 2011 }}</ref> and played from 10 to 20 December 2014.<ref name="schedule"/>

[[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] could not defend their title having been eliminated in the semi-finals of the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League]] by eventual winners [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]. The Spanish side went on to beat Mexico's [[Cruz Azul]] 4–0 in the Club World Cup semi-finals, before defeating [[San Lorenzo de Almagro|San Lorenzo]] 2–0 in the [[2014 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]] to give them their first Club World Cup title.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/round=259701/match=300298898/match-report.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141220234228/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/round=259701/match=300298898/match-report.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=20 December 2014|work=FIFA|title=Real Madrid coast to Morocco 2014 title|access-date=20 December 2014}}</ref> Including the [[1960 Intercontinental Cup|1960]], [[1998 Intercontinental Cup|1998]] and [[2002 Intercontinental Cup]]s, it was Real Madrid's fourth world club title, equalling [[A.C. Milan|Milan]]'s record.<ref>Regarding it as world club title de facto, cf. {{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2014/m=12/news=real-madrid-turn-their-winning-run-into-a-world-title-2495659.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141222031533/http://www.fifa.com//clubworldcup/news/y=2014/m=12/news=real-madrid-turn-their-winning-run-into-a-world-title-2495659.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=22 December 2014|work=FIFA|title=Real Madrid turn winning run into a world title|access-date=21 December 2014|quote=In terms of making history, Real, who have now equalled Milan's record of four World and Intercontinental Cup wins, ...}}<br/>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea6e105e132-ed9adc68e1ac-1000--real-madrid-claim-fifa-club-world-cup/|work=UEFA|title=Real Madrid claim FIFA Club World Cup|access-date=20 December 2014|quote=Madrid equal AC Milan's record of four world club titles, having both also lifted the old European-South American Cup three times before clinching the successor trophy, each under Carlo Ancelotti.}}</ref>

==Host bids==
There were four countries bidding to host the [[2013 FIFA Club World Cup|2013]] and 2014 tournaments (same host for both tournaments):<ref>{{cite news | url = https://www.usatoday.com/sports/soccer/2011-05-17-iran-morocco-south-africa-uae-fifa-club-world-cup-bids_N.htm | title = Iran among four bidders to host 2013-14 FIFA Club World Cups | publisher = USA Today | agency = Associated Press| date = 17 May 2011 }}</ref>
*{{fba|Iran}}
*{{fba|Morocco}}
*{{fba|South Africa}}
*{{fba|United Arab Emirates}} (which hosted the [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup|2009]] and [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup|2010]] editions in [[Abu Dhabi]])

In October 2011, [[FIFA]] said that Iran, South Africa and the United Arab Emirates all withdrew their bids, leaving Morocco as the only bidder.<ref>{{cite news | url = http://msn.foxsports.com/foxsoccer/soccer/story/Morocco-set-to-host-Club-World-Cup-in-2013-14-99039620 | title = Morocco set to host Club World Cup in 2013, '14 | publisher = FoxSports.com | agency = Associated Press| date = 17 October 2011 }}</ref> The [[FIFA Executive Committee]] officially confirmed Morocco as host on 17 December 2011 during their meeting in [[Tokyo]], Japan.<ref>{{cite web|title=Reform road map speeds up|url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/bodies/news/newsid=1558624/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120104113830/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/bodies/news/newsid=1558624/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=4 January 2012|publisher=FIFA|date=17 December 2011}}</ref>

On 21 August 2014, FIFA issued a statement reconfirming Morocco as the host, despite recent rumours that a change in venue might be sought due to the [[2014 West Africa Ebola virus outbreak]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/newsid=2424582/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140821171632/http://www.fifa.com//clubworldcup/news/newsid=2424582/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=21 August 2014|title=Morocco reconfirmed as FIFA Club World Cup venue|publisher=FIFA.com|date=21 August 2014}}</ref> Morocco had cancelled its hosting of the [[2015 Africa Cup of Nations]] due to fears of Ebola, but vowed to host the Club World Cup as no entrants would be from the countries with the most severe Ebola outbreaks.<ref>{{cite news|title=Africa Cup of Nations: Morocco will not host finals over Ebola fears|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30004279|access-date=11 November 2014|work=BBC Sport|date=11 November 2014}}</ref>

